What is the mode of this data set?', NULL, $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"$3$"},{"key":"B","text":"$4.33$"},{"key":"C","text":"$5$"},{"key":"D","text":"$6$"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'D', NULL, E'The mode is the value that appears most frequently. The value $6$ appears three times, more than any other value. The value $3$ appears twice, which is the second most frequent but not the mode. The value $4.33$ is approximately the mean ($\\frac{39}{9} \\approx 4.33$), which confuses mean with mode. The value $5$ is the median (the $5$th value in the ordered list), which confuses median with mode.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"concept_gap"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"misread_question"},"D":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 45, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-27"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-27","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);











- The baobab tree (Adansonia digitata) is native to sub-Saharan Africa and can live for over 1,500 years.
- Its trunk can store up to 120,000 liters of water, which communities tap during dry seasons.
- Baobab fruit pulp contains six times more vitamin C than oranges and is a staple dietary supplement in rural Senegal and Mali.
- The tree''s bark fibers are woven into rope and cloth.
- Several baobab specimens in Madagascar have become tourist attractions.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"The baobab tree, native to sub-Saharan Africa, can live for over 1,500 years and has become a tourist attraction in Madagascar."},{"key":"B","text":"Communities in sub-Saharan Africa rely on the baobab tree for water stored in its trunk during dry seasons, vitamin-C-rich fruit pulp used as a dietary supplement, and bark fibers woven into rope and cloth."},{"key":"C","text":"Baobab fruit pulp contains six times more vitamin C than oranges, making the baobab tree one of the most remarkable species in sub-Saharan Africa."},{"key":"D","text":"Native to sub-Saharan Africa, the baobab tree is known for its massive trunk, which can store up to 120,000 liters of water."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'The goal is to highlight the baobab''s practical importance to local communities. The choice beginning with "Communities in sub-Saharan Africa rely on" directly names three distinct community uses — water access, dietary supplementation, and fiber for rope and cloth — making it the most effective option for the stated goal. The choice mentioning the tree''s age and tourist status in Madagascar focuses on longevity and tourism rather than practical community uses. The choice about vitamin C content mentions only one use and shifts to a general evaluative claim ("most remarkable species") without connecting the fruit to community practice. The choice describing the trunk''s water storage capacity provides only one practical detail and frames it as a botanical fact rather than emphasizing community reliance.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 75, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":